    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "A PROCESS grouping in which geomorphic UNITS are biogenic landforms (bioconstructions and build-ups) that can predominantly be attributed to the activity of organisms at or below highest astronomical tides.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/back_reef",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:back_reef"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:back_reef"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "back reef (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "Wide (up to kms) zone extending back from the REEF FLAT, mainly found in linear-shaped reefs. BACK REEF zone supports scattered small submerged PATCH REEFS and extensive sand bank development (Maxwell, 1968).",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/biogenic_reef",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:biogenic_reef"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:biogenic_reef"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "biogenic reef (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "In-situ, positive relief, persistent build-ups of primarily skeleton-supported framework (+ internal binding), that influence the local sedimentary environment (Klement, 1967), and supports (or supported) living communities during active accretion. Definition modified from a range of sources: (Cumings, 1932; Goudie, 2006; Harris and Baker, 2020; Klement, 1967; Lo Iacono et al., 2018).Cf. REEF (MARINE SETTING).",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/bioherm",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:bioherm"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:bioherm"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "bioherm (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "Build-ups with lens-like positive relief (Cumings, 1932), with variable skeletal and matrix components (± binding); skeletal components are adjacent and some may be in contact, but are mostly disarticulated (modified from Riding, 2002). (e.g., <I>Halimeda</I> bioherm). Synonym ‘mound’; includes carbonate ‘mud mound’ (modified from Riding, 2002). Usually parautochthonous to allochthonous.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/biostrome",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:biostrome"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:biostrome"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "biostrome (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "Build-ups lacking the lens-like positive relief that would otherwise classify the unit as a bioherm (Cumings, 1932); bedded or unbedded (Kershaw, 1994) with variable skeletal and matrix components. Synonym ‘bed’ (Kershaw, 1994). Usually parautochthonous to allochthonous.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/excavation",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:excavation"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:excavation"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "excavation (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "A mappable unit representing sediment transport or reworking by the activity of living benthic infauna and epifauna (bioturbation) (modified from Kristensen et al., 2012). Often mapped as a FIELD, rather than individual components, including (not limited to) feeding trace, nest, burrow, boring, resting site, track, trail, mound.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/fore-reef",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:fore-reef"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:fore-reef"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "fore-reef (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "The outside slope of a coral REEF seaward of the REEF CREST (or reef edge) facing open sea (Cabioch, 2011; Maxwell, 1968).",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/mat",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:mat"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:mat"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "mat (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "A sheet-like organic and detrital-rich accumulation generated from baffling and trapping (± binding) by macrophytes (e.g. seagrass), filamentous algae and cyanobacteria to form an organo-sedimentary build-up. Examples include ‘algal mat’ and ‘seagrass bed’. Usually allochthonous.",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/reef_crest",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:reef_crest"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:reef_crest"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "reef crest (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "Reef top behind the FORE-REEF, comprising an elevated rim and hard coralgal pavement which attenuates wave energy (Maxwell, 1968).",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/reef_flat",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:reef_flat"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:reef_flat"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "reef flat (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "The most recent expression of coral growth at sea level, extending back from the FORE-REEF. There are two forms of REEF FLATS: (1) coral dominated and (2) rubble-dominated, modulated by the ambient energy conditions as the growing reef approaches sea-level (Maxwell, 1968; Thornborough and Davies, 2011).",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/reef_lagoon",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:reef_lagoon"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:reef_lagoon"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "reef lagoon (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "Fully or partly enclosed shallow depressions in coral REEFs (Hopley, 2011; Maxwell, 1968).",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "https://pid.geoscience.gov.au/def/voc/ga/SeabedGeomorphologySettings/spur-and-groove",
    "https://prez.dev/identifier": [
      {
        "@value": "ns1:spur-and-groove"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/link": [
      {
        "@value": "/catalogs/catalogue:default/collections/ns1:SeabedGeomorphologySettings/items/ns1:spur-and-groove"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/label": [
      {
        "@value": "spur-and-groove (BGU)",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ],
    "https://prez.dev/description": [
      {
        "@value": "Best developed on the windward side of coral REEFS, the spur-and-grooves consists of parallel elongate CHANNELS (MORPHOLOGY) or GROOVES (MORPHOLOGY), a few metres wide and deep, separated by seaward extending coral RIDGES (MORPHOLOGY) or spurs (Harris and Baker, 2020; Maxwell, 1968; Shinn, 2011).",
        "@language": "en"
      }
    ]
  },
